.TH AM 1 "November 2024" "2.1.0" "Alias Manager Manual"
.SH NAME
am \- manage shell aliases efficiently
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B am
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B am
is a lightweight command-line tool for managing shell aliases. It provides a simple interface to add, remove, and list aliases stored in
.IR ~/.my_aliases.txt .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "init " [\fIshell\fR]
Output shell configuration for the specified shell (bash, zsh, or fish).
If no shell is specified, auto-detects from the $SHELL environment variable.
Use with
.BR "eval \"$(am init)\"" .
.TP
.BR "add " \fIname\fR " " \fIcommand\fR...
Create or update an alias. If an alias with the same name exists, it will be replaced.
.TP
.BR "rm " \fIname\fR " " [\fI-f\fR]
Remove an alias. Without
.IR -f ,
prompts for confirmation.
.TP
.BR "ls " [\fIpattern\fR]
List all aliases, or filter by pattern if provided.
.TP
.B help
Show help message.
.TP
.B version
Show version information.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help message (same as
.BR "help" ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information (same as
.BR "version" ).
.TP
.B \-f
Force operation without confirmation (for
.BR rm " command)."
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.my_aliases.txt
Default file where aliases are stored.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Setup shell integration (run once):
.B eval "$(am init)"
.TP
Create an alias for git log:
.B am add gl git log \-\-oneline \-\-graph
.TP
List all aliases:
.B am ls
.TP
Search for git-related aliases:
.B am ls git
.TP
Remove an alias with confirmation:
.B am rm gl
.TP
Remove an alias without confirmation:
.B am rm gl \-f
.TP
Setup for a specific shell:
.B am init bash >> ~/.bashrc
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments
.TP
.B 2
File access error
.TP
.B 3
Home directory not found
.TP
.B 4
System error
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
The recommended way to integrate with your shell is to use the init command:
.PP
.RS
.B eval "$(am init)"
.RE
.PP
To make it permanent, add this line to your shell configuration file:
.PP
.RS
.B Bash:
echo 'eval "$(am init)"' >> ~/.bashrc
.br
.B Zsh:
echo 'eval "$(am init)"' >> ~/.zshrc
.br
.B Fish:
am init fish >> ~/.config/fish/config.fish
.RE
